# The semantic extension

`semantic` ranks a corpus against a query by the meaning a language model found
in both. The model is not in this binary and will not be: an index is built
ahead of time by [`contrib/semantics/`](../../../../contrib/semantics/README.md),
and this extension reads it.

```hql
import semantic

cards
| semantic("night hunting birds")
| take(3)
| table
```

Against the birds corpus that returns the owls, none of which contains the word
"night" — they are nocturnal, and abroad after dark. [[lexical]] over the same
query returns a swan, a songbird order and a kingfisher family, because shared
spellings are all it has.

## The index

A vault names its index, and the path resolves inside the vault root:

```toml
[semantics]
index = ".hql/index.sqlite"
```

With no index configured, `semantic` is an error naming both the missing
configuration and `hql-semantics build`. It never falls back to [[lexical]]: a
silent fallback restores exactly the confusion that renaming the matcher
removed, and a `Hit` would once again claim a model that had not run.

The index is untrusted input — a file the vault names, parsed by this binary —
so everything read from it is checked. An unknown schema version is refused
rather than interpreted. An index whose `index_meta.vault` names another corpus
is refused rather than used to rank cards it has never seen. Dimensions are
bounded, and a vector that is not exactly its declared length is refused rather
than read past.

## Why a query must be in the index

A consumer with no model cannot embed a document, and it cannot embed a question
either. So the producer embeds the queries too, and `semantic` looks one up. A
query the index has no vector for is a failure naming
`hql-semantics build --query "…"`, and never a ranking of nothing.

That is a real limit: this implementation answers the questions its index was
built for. It is the price of keeping the model out of the binary, and it is
paid openly — the alternative is a consumer that invents a vector and reports a
score nobody can reproduce.

## Staleness and coverage

Neither is silent and neither is fatal; both go through the
[report queue](../error-reporting.hmd).

- A card whose text has changed since the build has a vector for text that no
  longer exists. It **ranks anyway**, with a warning naming it. A stale score is
  a wrong answer rather than an impossible one — and skipping it would make an
  edited card vanish from a ranking, which a reader cannot tell from
  irrelevance.
- A card absent from the index is **absent from the ranking**, with one warning
  counting how many were skipped. Scoring it zero would be indistinguishable
  from indexed and unrelated.

## What a ranking reports

Every `Hit` carries the `Retrieval`: the query, the index path, the model id,
its pinned `revision`, `metric: cosine`, and `approximate: false` — scoring is
exact brute force over every stored vector, with no approximate index and no
top-k pushdown. See [[hql/semantic-search]] for why `approximate` is a field
rather than an assumption, and
[HQL-0002](../../../proposals/HQL-0002/README.md) for the schema, the text
contract and what is out of scope.
